+### R-22 What last ping, next ping and the ping counter mean
+Status: baseline · Source: observed (`hc/api/models.py` `ping`); tested (`hc/api/tests/test_ping.py` "test_start_endpoint_works"); tested (`hc/api/tests/test_ping.py` "test_log_endpoint_works") · As of: 9f12cd8d
+- `last_ping` is the time of the latest success or failure signal (a plain ping, a `/fail` ping, an email ping, or a ping with an exit status above zero) and is `null` for a check that has none. (`hc/api/models.py` `self.last_ping = frozen_now`)
+- Start signals, log signals and ignored pings leave `last_ping` unchanged but still raise `n_pings`. (`hc/api/tests/test_ping.py` "test_start_endpoint_works")
+- Among the pings that are ignored (recorded, but changing no status): any ping to a check that is paused with manual resume on, a request that is not a POST to a check limited to POST, and a ping whose body or subject matches no keyword filter when the check does not default to failure. (`hc/api/tests/test_ping.py` "test_it_requires_post")
+- Resuming a paused check (web or API) and clearing a check's events reset `last_ping` to `null`; pausing keeps it. (`hc/api/views.py` `check.last_ping = None`)
+- `next_ping` is the time the grace time begins, and `null` for new, paused and down checks; a late check that the alert process has not yet recorded as down still shows its past due time, and while a start signal is pending the start time can show instead (read from the code, not covered by a test). (`hc/api/tests/test_list_checks.py` "test_it_works")
+- A ping, by HTTP or by email, updates the check and stores its log entry in one transaction under a row lock, so a reader never sees the new `last_ping` without the new log entry. (`hc/api/models.py` `select_for_update`)
